{"qid":"Q157727","names":["Ifat Sultanate"],"title":"Ifat Sultanate","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Sultanate of Ifat was a medieval Muslim state in the eastern Horn of Africa, centred in the Shewa region of modern Ethiopia, and ruled by the Walashma dynasty from around 1285. It was the foremost Islamic power in the region and repeatedly clashed with the Christian Solomonic emperors of Abyssinia, most notably in the campaigns of Amda Seyon I (r. 1314–1344), who defeated Sultan Haqq ad-Din I and subjugated Ifat in 1328–29. The sultanate was absorbed and transformed after continued Abyssinian pressure in the early 15th century, with the Walashma dynasty relocating eastward to found the Adal Sultanate.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The territory of Ifat is poorly delineated in the sources, with its extent reconstructed from Ge'ez royal chronicles and a small number of Arabic documents; map representations are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Sultanate of Ifat","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultanate_of_Ifat"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Amda Seyon I","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amda_Seyon_I"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Walashma dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walashma_dynasty"}],"britannica":"place/Ifat","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Sultanate of Ifat","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Umar ibn Dunya-Hawaz (Umar Walashma)","nativeName":"عمر بن دنيا هوز","roman":"Umar Dunya-Hawaz","start":1285,"end":1298,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Ali \"Baziwi\" ibn Umar","nativeName":"علي بازيوي بن عمر","roman":"Ali Baziwi","start":1298,"end